{
  "id": "80698",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Titration",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": " In the 18th century, the concentration of vinegar samples was determined by noting the amount of potassium carbonate, K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " , which had to be added, a little at a time, before bubbling ceased. The greater the weight of potassium carbonate added to reach the point where the bubbling ended, the more concentrated the vinegar. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "b385b236d63f45178ddedcaee905cb5c",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": " We now know that the effervescence that occurred during this process was due to reaction with acetic acid, CH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "H, the compound primarily responsible for the odor and taste of vinegar. Acetic acid reacts with potassium carbonate according to the following equation: "
              }
            ],
            "id": "a0b7f09011da41889cd9f1c365628289",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "h0hvcq57d6kxto5",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "odktbdcnrslka51",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm{CH}}_3{\\mathrm{CO}}_2\\mathrm H(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm K}_2{\\mathrm{CO}}_3(s)\\;\\longrightarrow\\;{\\mathrm{KCH}}_3{\\mathrm{CO}}_3(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm{CO}}_2(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": " The bubbling was due to the production of CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": " gas. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "be09f03845b942229089e224b301fb0f",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The test of vinegar with potassium carbonate is one type of quantitative analysis—the determination of the amount or concentration of a substance in a sample. In the analysis of vinegar, the concentration of the solute (acetic acid) was determined from the amount of reactant that combined with the solute present in a known volume of the solution. In other types of chemical analyses, the amount of a substance present in a sample is determined by measuring the amount of product that results."
              }
            ],
            "id": "feccebd7f871459daf68f44854c97ff8",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The described approach to measuring vinegar strength was an early version of the analytical technique known as "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "titration analysis"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A quantitative chemical analysis method that involves measuring the volume of a reactant solution required to completely react with the analyte in a sample."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1928633369",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". A typical titration analysis involves the use of a "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "buret"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A device used for the precise delivery of variable liquid volumes, such as in a titration analysis."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1528187889",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " to make incremental additions of a solution containing a known concentration of some substance (the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "titrant"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A solution containing a known concentration of substance that will react with the analyte in a titration analysis."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1824186023",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ") to a sample solution containing the substance whose concentration is to be measured (the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "analyte"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The chemical species of interest."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4231590696",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": "). The titrant and analyte undergo a chemical reaction of known stoichiometry, and so measuring the volume of titrant solution required for complete reaction with the analyte (the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "equivalence point"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The volume of titrant solution required to react completely with the analyte in a titration analysis; provides a stoichiometric amount of titrant for the sample’s analyte according to the titration reaction."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "986879340",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of the titration) allows calculation of the analyte concentration."
              }
            ],
            "id": "c96ffcbc2baa42e3998c5216cb12601e",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The equivalence point of a titration may be detected visually if a distinct change in the appearance of the sample solution accompanies the completion of the reaction. The halt of bubble formation in the classic vinegar analysis is one such example, though, more commonly, a special dye called an "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "indicator"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A substance added to the sample in a titration analysis to permit visual detection of the end point."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "310040096",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is added to the sample solution to impart a change in color at or very near the equivalence point of the titration. Equivalence points may also be detected by measuring some solution property that changes in a predictable way during the course of the titration. Regardless of the approach taken to detect a titration’s equivalence point, the volume of titrant actually measured is called the end point. Properly designed titration methods typically ensure that the difference between the equivalence and end points is negligible. Though any type of chemical reaction may serve as the basis for a titration analysis, precipitation, acid–base, and redox are the most common."
              }
            ],
            "id": "ea84e4acf213464fa228a82bac5b9752",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "q8u0r2ixwqqw2a9",
                "text": "Slide Show"
              }
            ],
            "id": "bmocrbzw2ky6buf",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " ",
